    "['network [ip_address] [wildcard_mask] area [area_id]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ospf area 0 interface lo0.0', 'parent_command': ['set protocols ospf area [parameter1]', 'interface [parameter2]'], 'root': 'set protocols ospf area [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco OSPF network command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ospf area 0 interface lo0.0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Cisco command `network 1.1.1.1 0.0.0.0 area 0` specifies that the exact interface with IP 1.1.1.1 should be included in OSPF area 0\\n- In Juniper, we directly configure OSPF on the loopback interface (typically lo0.0) and assign it to area 0\\n- The Juniper command achieves the same result of advertising the loopback interface in OSPF area 0\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols ospf area 0 interface lo0.0'}]]": 1,
    "['network [network_address] [wildcard_mask] area [area_id]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ospf area 0 interface ge-1/0/2.0', 'parent_command': ['set protocols ospf area [parameter1]', 'interface [parameter2]'], 'root': 'set protocols ospf area [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco OSPF network configuration command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ospf area 0 interface ge-1/0/2.0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. The Cisco command `network 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0` enables OSPF on the interface with IP address 10.1.1.2 (from the config) and places it in area 0\\n2. In Juniper, we directly specify the interface (ge-1/0/2) rather than using network statements\\n3. The `.0` suffix is Juniper's notation for unit/interface numbering\\n4. The command enables OSPF on that interface in area 0\\n\\nNote: The exact interface name might vary slightly depending on Juniper platform (e.g., might be `ge-1/0/2` or `ge-1/0/2.0`).\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols ospf area 0 interface ge-1/0/2.0'}]]": 1,
